## What to return

**What you are looking for is results.** A result is something the document asserts to be so: a
theorem, a relation it derives, a bound, a condition under which something holds, a conclusion it
draws from an argument. Record what the document states rather than only what it proves formally:
a result stated in plain prose is a result. What is not a result, however assertive its sentences
read: motivation (why a question matters), signposting (what the document or a later section will
do), and recap (a result already stated, here or in another document, repeated or summarised).

Your answer is a sequence of blocks, each opened and closed by its two markers on lines of their
own. Nothing numbers them and nothing in one refers to another. What sits between a block's two
markers is taken exactly as it stands: write backslashes, dollars and braces as you would write
them, and escape nothing.

### Claim blocks

Each result is one claim block, unless it begins inside a paragraph listed under *Paragraph
verdicts* below.

**You name where a result begins; the tool works out how far it runs.** A result runs from the
sentence you name to the next sentence you name in the same paragraph, or to the end of that
paragraph. So name the sentence each result begins at; how much of the document a result covers
is not something the answer carries.

Each claim block carries three lines:

- **`quote:`** the opening of the sentence the result begins at, copied from the body above in the
  document's own words — around ten words, enough that no other sentence opens the same way.
  Leave the label and its colon out of it: they are this rendering's and not the document's. The
  quote is how the tool finds the sentence, and never what it records.
- **`label:`** the label of that same sentence. The tool resolves the quote itself and reads this
  as a cross-check, so a quote and a label naming two different sentences is an answer it cannot
  use.
- **`title:`** yours to write: one line naming the result. It is a title and never a heading — no
  leading `#`, no numbering, no markup. No two titles in your answer may be the same, and none may
  repeat the title of a claim the document already carries.

A structural label standing on its own — `Proof.`, `Definition 3.` — is not a sentence a result
begins at.

